== 1.1 What the Subject Means

*Physical education* (PE) is an educational process that uses movement and physical activities to help learners develop knowledge, skills and attitudes for participation and wellbeing. It can teach a child to move safely, understand rules, cooperate and make informed choices. A PE lesson is not limited to elite competition.

*Health education* helps people develop knowledge and skills to make informed decisions about health. It may address personal hygiene, nutrition, disease prevention and access to appropriate care. Knowledge matters, but a person's choices are also affected by opportunity, resources, environment and support.

*Physical activity* is bodily movement produced by skeletal muscles that requires energy expenditure. Walking to school, playing and household tasks can be physical activity. *Exercise* is planned, structured and repeated physical activity undertaken to improve or maintain fitness. *Sport* generally involves activities governed by rules and often organised practice or competition; definitions and local contexts can vary.

#rule-box("An overlap, not a list of opposites")[
  A football practice can be sport, exercise and physical activity at the same time. A PE lesson can include a game without making competition its only educational purpose.
]

== 1.2 Aims and Learning Outcomes

A well-designed programme can support several kinds of learning. A *physical or motor* outcome concerns movement skills and physical capacities; a *cognitive* outcome concerns understanding, such as explaining a rule or a safe warm-up. A *social* outcome can include cooperation and respect for others. An *emotional* outcome may include confidence or learning to respond constructively to success and disappointment.

These categories overlap. A learner practising a pass can improve coordination while communicating with a teammate and learning a rule. Outcomes should be appropriate to the learner and setting; a lesson should not be judged solely by the score in a match.

