== 1.3 Participation, Safety and Inclusion

Activities should be suited to participants' age, ability, experience and health circumstances. A teacher or coach can modify space, equipment, rules or pace to make an activity accessible while preserving its purpose. Adaptation is not the same as assuming that all people with the same diagnosis have identical needs; ask the participant and follow relevant guidance.

Check the playing area and equipment, provide appropriate supervision and allow time for suitable preparation and recovery. Participants should report symptoms or injuries rather than being pressured to continue. A person with a medical condition or new concerning symptoms may need individual professional advice; this study guide does not replace that advice.

#concept-box("Include the learner")[
  An inclusive lesson offers meaningful ways to participate. For example, a teacher may change the size of a playing area or use a suitable ball, then review whether the change works for the people involved.
]

== 1.4 People and Settings

A physical education teacher plans and assesses learning; a coach helps participants develop skills and performance in a particular activity; a health educator supports learning about health. Responsibilities can overlap and depend on training, policy and local rules. A first-aid responder gives help within their training while arranging further assistance when needed; they do not replace emergency services or a clinician.

Schools, homes, sports clubs and communities all influence opportunities for activity. Cost, accessible facilities, transport, safety and available time can affect participation. Good programmes consider these barriers rather than blaming a person for every limitation.

== 1.5 Four Worked Examples
#worked-example(1, "Identify exercise", tier: "FOUNDATION",
  [Which activity best illustrates planned exercise? \
A. A scheduled walking session undertaken to improve fitness \
B. An accidental fall \
C. A paper examination \
D. A printer producing a report],
  [The walk is planned and undertaken to improve fitness. It is exercise and also physical activity. The answer is *A*.])
#worked-example(2, "A learning outcome", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A learner explains why a warm-up is used before activity. This is most directly a \
A. purely equipment outcome \
B. cognitive outcome \
C. bone fracture \
D. competition result],
  [Explaining a principle demonstrates understanding, a cognitive outcome. The answer is *B*.])
#worked-example(3, "Adapt an activity", tier: "UTME STANDARD",
  [A teacher changes a game's space and equipment so that a learner can participate meaningfully. This is an example of \
A. automatic exclusion \
B. treating every learner identically regardless of need \
C. inclusive adaptation \
D. replacing all safety checks],
  [The teacher modifies the activity to support meaningful participation. The answer is *C*.])
#worked-example(4, "Overlapping categories", tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
  [A scheduled football training session is organised under rules and intended to improve fitness. It can be \
A. only health education \
B. neither physical activity nor sport \
C. a sport but never exercise \
D. physical activity, exercise and sport],
  [The session involves movement, is structured for fitness and is an organised sport activity. The categories overlap. The answer is *D*.])

#trap-box("Four distinctions")[
  Activity is not always planned exercise. Sport is not the whole of PE. Knowing a health fact does not remove every environmental barrier. Inclusion is not the same as making every learner perform an identical task.
]

